.venv/
__pycache__/
*.egg-info/
# What `pip install addons/<package>` leaves in the package directory: pip builds a local
# directory IN TREE, so `setup.py`'s overlay copy (`addons/overlay_build.py`) lands under
# `build/lib/` next to the pyproject and would be a second, stale copy of the core's modules
# waiting for `git add -A`.
addons/*/build/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/
*.pyc

# scratch & logs
scratch-*
*.log
# The mutation runner's crash marker (tools/mutate.py). It exists to survive a SIGKILL, so it is
# ON DISK during every mutation round — and `git add -A` is the command every session here runs.
# It was committed by accident in 015f806 carrying TWO absolute paths out of the maintainer's home
# directory, which is the same disclosure the `.coverage` entry below was added for: a login name
# reaching a public clone. The marker is a note to the next process on this machine, never content.
.mutate-in-flight
.openfactory-worktrees/
.openfactory-logs/

# secrets — never commit
.env
.env.*
# …EXCEPT the templates, which are the opposite of a secret: they are what somebody clones the
# repository to copy. `!.env.example` used to name ONE file, so `.env.compose.example` — the file
# docker-compose.yml's first line tells you to copy — was silently absent from the distribution.
# A pattern, not a name, so the next template is covered without anybody remembering.
!.env*.example
.secrets/
*.pem

# spike / POC (throwaway)
spike/

# scratchpad — local working files & credentials (aws.env etc.); NEVER commit
scratchpad/

# terraform — never commit state (may hold secrets) or local provider cache
**/.terraform/
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*
*.tfplan
.terraform.tfstate.lock.info

# per-deployment terraform values (real secrets/IDs — never commit; use .example as the template)
infra/terraform/deployment.tfvars
infra/terraform/*.auto.tfvars

# per-deployment project registry (real project + Slack coordinates baked into the worker
# image — never commit; use registry.yaml.example as the template)
deploy/registry.yaml

# local agent scratch (worktrees, session state) — never part of the product
.claude/

# setuptools' build tree. It was TRACKED — 201 stale copies of `sdlc/` swept in by a `git add -A`
# — and that is worse than untidy: `test_the_wheel_ships_what_the_platform_needs` exists because
# an in-tree build reuses `build/lib/` and ships files the CURRENT configuration no longer
# selects, so a committed copy is a second, older source tree that a reader (and a grep, and a
# licence scanner on a public repo) can mistake for the real one.
build/
*.egg-info/

# A CLIENT'S OWN DOCUMENTS, KEPT ONLY TO BE REVISITED — never committed. `docs/temp/` is where a
# client's architecture lands while we are reasoning about it: Key Vault URLs, SharePoint site
# addresses, environment maps, internal gaps. The product owner, 2026-08-08: *"quando falei persistir é para
# caso precisemos revisitar o tema"* — a scratch pad, not a deliverable.
#
# IGNORED RATHER THAN REMEMBERED, and the entry above says why in the same words: this repository
# has already had 201 stale files swept in by a `git add -A`, and that is the command every
# session here runs. The difference is the blast radius — git history is permanent, and this tree
# is published as `openfactory-core`. A client's internal addresses reaching a public repository
# is not untidy, it is a disclosure.
#
# What survives instead is the CONCLUSION: whatever these documents change about the plan goes
# into the card it changes, which is where anyone would look to revisit it anyway.
docs/temp/

# macOS Finder litter
.DS_Store
tests/.failures.log

# Coverage artefact — a binary SQLite file carrying ABSOLUTE paths from whoever ran the
# suite. It was tracked by accident, and in an open-source repository that publishes the
# maintainer's home directory to every clone (2026-08-17).
.coverage
.coverage.*

# THE REAL IDENTITY LIST, AND THE MUTATION PLANS THAT PLANT IT. The two identity guards used to
# carry the names they forbid — a login, an address, client names, two live account ids — and on
# 2026-08-25 `git grep` over the tree found them ONLY there: the guard was the disclosure. The
# mechanism ships with synthetic shapes (tests/identity_forbidden.py); the real names live in this
# file on the maintainers' machines and are unioned in when present — OPTIONAL on every run, since
# a fork never holds it and a test that needs a secret is a red CI on every fork's pull request.
# Same for a mutation plan that puts a real name back into the tree to prove the guard bites: run
# by path, never committed.
tests/.identity-forbidden.txt
tools/mutations/local/
